{"id":"cigarette_smoke","compound_name":"Graphs::Aero::cigarette_smoke","source_path":"cigarette_smoke/cigarette_smoke.json","category":"Smoke","author":"Autodesk","description":"An aero simulation producing cigarette type smoke","documentation_file":"cigarette_smoke_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Cigarette Smoke Graph\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph uses aero to create a cigarette smoke type simulation.\n\n### Inputs and outputs \n\nInputs for this graph are the meshes representing the tips of cigarettes (the \"cherry\").  The output is the resulting aero volume from the smoke simulation.  \n\n### Useful information\n\n- To get the soft silky look of cigarette smoke, solver `style` is set to smooth, and `velocity smoothness` is turned up.  Since cigarette smoke is usually quite faint, `kill voxel threshold` is reduced to allow for voxels with very low fog density.  Also, variations for `temperature` and `fog_density` are used at sourcing time to break uniformity and create details.\n\n- Cigarette smoke simulation is highly dependent on scene scale, since very small emitters are required to produce the correct behavior.  Please adjust the `scene units in meters` on the solver settings accordingly, or scale your geometry.\n\n- Performance is directly related to the `fluid detail size` on the source air node, and the desired length of life for the smoke, determined by dissipation. \n\n### Example scene\n\n- The example scenes puts this effect in context, with animated geometry, and collisions set on the cigarette body for a more accurate representation.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"out_geometry","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"},{"portName":"sphere_mesh","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal